1
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:05,593
Everybody tells you the algorithm decides who wins.

2
00:00:06,007 --> 00:00:11,449
I spent four years believing that, and it cost me about eleven

3
00:00:11,509 --> 00:00:14,340
thousand hours of work I would like back.

4
00:00:15,616 --> 00:00:17,407
Here is what actually happened.

5
00:00:17,856 --> 00:00:21,320
In 2021 I was posting five times a week.

6
00:00:21,556 --> 00:00:27,512
Every video was better than the last one by any measure I could

7
00:00:27,572 --> 00:00:30,782
name. Better lighting, better audio, tighter edit.

8
00:00:31,108 --> 00:00:33,580
And the channel did not move.

9
00:00:33,851 --> 00:00:37,596
Eight hundred subscribers in January, nine hundred and twelve in

10
00:00:37,656 --> 00:00:38,556
December.

11
00:00:39,843 --> 00:00:41,975
So I did what everyone does.

12
00:00:42,274 --> 00:00:44,039
I blamed the distribution.

13
00:00:44,350 --> 00:00:48,342
I read every thread about watch time and session duration and click-through

14
00:00:48,402 --> 00:00:50,660
rate. I bought two courses.

15
00:00:51,097 --> 00:00:53,996
I rewrote my titles about forty times.

16
00:00:55,463 --> 00:00:59,438
And then in March of the following year I stopped uploading for

17
00:00:59,498 --> 00:01:01,909
six weeks. Not as a strategy.

18
00:01:02,252 --> 00:01:07,453
I was just tired. When I came back I made one video that I did

19
00:01:07,513 --> 00:01:12,576
not care about at all. It was fourteen minutes of me complaining

20
00:01:12,636 --> 00:01:14,606
about a piece of software.

21
00:01:14,945 --> 00:01:18,007
No thumbnail plan. I filmed it on a Tuesday.

22
00:01:18,974 --> 00:01:21,953
That video did two hundred and forty thousand views.

23
00:01:22,312 --> 00:01:26,943
And the reason is the thing nobody wants to hear, because it is

24
00:01:27,003 --> 00:01:29,795
not a tactic you can buy.

25
00:01:30,660 --> 00:01:36,093
The reason is that for the first time in four years I said something

26
00:01:36,153 --> 00:01:40,132
that was actually true and slightly costly to say.

27
00:01:40,568 --> 00:01:44,664
Everything before that had been optimized into a shape that offended

28
00:01:44,724 --> 00:01:46,582
nobody and interested nobody.

29
00:01:47,558 --> 00:01:52,193
Let me be specific about what I mean, because "be authentic" is

30
00:01:52,253 --> 00:01:55,825
useless advice and I hate hearing it too.

31
00:01:57,143 --> 00:02:00,314
There is a measurable thing happening here.

32
00:02:00,780 --> 00:02:05,318
When you watch a video, you decide within about two seconds whether

33
00:02:05,378 --> 00:02:08,610
to keep watching. Not two seconds of the hook.

34
00:02:09,018 --> 00:02:11,078
Two seconds of the person.

35
00:02:11,453 --> 00:02:15,465
And what you are measuring in that window is whether this person

36
00:02:15,525 --> 00:02:20,654
is going to tell you something they would be slightly embarrassed

37
00:02:20,714 --> 00:02:23,255
to say at a dinner party.

38
00:02:24,090 --> 00:02:27,033
That is the whole thing. That is retention.

39
00:02:27,969 --> 00:02:30,296
Most creators optimize for the opposite.

40
00:02:30,757 --> 00:02:34,421
They optimize for the thing that is safe to say.

41
00:02:34,855 --> 00:02:38,787
And safe things have a property: they are already known.

42
00:02:39,178 --> 00:02:44,969
If it is already known, there is no reason to keep watching, because

43
00:02:45,029 --> 00:02:48,111
the payoff is already in your head.

44
00:02:49,268 --> 00:02:52,500
Okay. So how do you actually operationalize that.

45
00:02:52,876 --> 00:02:58,732
Because I am not going to sit here and tell you to be brave.

46
00:03:00,075 --> 00:03:06,879
The first thing I do now, before I write a single line of a script,

47
00:03:06,939 --> 00:03:12,546
is I write down the sentence I do not want to say.

48
00:03:12,831 --> 00:03:17,492
Just one. The thing that if my most annoying viewer heard it, they

49
00:03:17,552 --> 00:03:21,579
would screenshot it. And then I build the video around it.

50
00:03:22,824 --> 00:03:24,193
Not because controversy works.

51
00:03:24,427 --> 00:03:29,421
Controversy is a different thing and it burns your channel down.

52
00:03:29,908 --> 00:03:34,827
But because the sentence you do not want to say is the only reliable

53
00:03:34,887 --> 00:03:37,609
signal you have that you are saying something.

54
00:03:38,832 --> 00:03:43,933
The second thing, and this is the boring one, is that I cut the

55
00:03:43,993 --> 00:03:47,966
first thirty seconds of every video after I finish editing it.

56
00:03:48,353 --> 00:03:50,004
Every single one. No exceptions.

57
00:03:51,207 --> 00:03:54,400
I did this as an experiment for three months.

58
00:03:54,914 --> 00:03:58,049
Average view duration went from thirty-one percent to forty-eight

59
00:03:58,109 --> 00:04:00,906
percent. That is not a small change.

60
00:04:01,423 --> 00:04:06,169
That is the difference between a video that gets shown to your

61
00:04:06,229 --> 00:04:09,338
subscribers and a video that gets shown to strangers.

62
00:04:10,473 --> 00:04:13,583
And it works for a stupid reason.

63
00:04:13,965 --> 00:04:18,796
The first thirty seconds of anything you film is you warming up.

64
00:04:19,078 --> 00:04:21,368
You know it is you warming up.

65
00:04:21,764 --> 00:04:25,865
You cut it anyway because you are attached to the setup.

66
00:04:26,300 --> 00:04:28,591
Do not be attached to the setup.

67
00:04:29,755 --> 00:04:34,573
Now here is where it gets interesting, and this is the part I actually

68
00:04:34,633 --> 00:04:36,648
wanted to talk about today.

69
00:04:37,791 --> 00:04:40,931
I started applying the same idea to the clips.

70
00:04:41,224 --> 00:04:44,653
Because at some point every channel becomes two channels.

71
00:04:44,995 --> 00:04:49,982
You have the long-form thing and then you have the short vertical

72
00:04:50,042 --> 00:04:54,013
thing, and the short vertical thing is where all the new people

73
00:04:54,073 --> 00:04:55,202
come from now.

74
00:04:56,223 --> 00:04:58,987
And clipping is genuinely miserable work.

75
00:04:59,443 --> 00:05:05,003
I timed it. For a forty-five minute episode, finding six good clips,

76
00:05:05,063 --> 00:05:08,527
trimming them, writing hooks, writing titles for three platforms,

77
00:05:08,587 --> 00:05:12,179
timing the captions, and scheduling them takes me somewhere between

78
00:05:12,239 --> 00:05:14,332
four and six hours. Every week.

79
00:05:14,836 --> 00:05:15,736
Forever.

80
00:05:17,218 --> 00:05:22,188
So I tried to automate it and I failed three times, and the way

81
00:05:22,248 --> 00:05:23,573
I failed is instructive.

82
00:05:24,555 --> 00:05:30,395
The first attempt, I just asked a language model to find the good

83
00:05:30,455 --> 00:05:34,835
parts. And it did something very reasonable and completely useless.

84
00:05:35,237 --> 00:05:37,648
It found the parts that summarized well.

85
00:05:38,147 --> 00:05:41,218
Which is the opposite of what you want.

86
00:05:41,583 --> 00:05:46,677
A summary is a thing that removes the reason to watch.

87
00:05:47,859 --> 00:05:50,840
The second attempt, I used engagement data.

88
00:05:51,306 --> 00:05:55,423
I looked at where the retention graph spiked in the long-form video

89
00:05:55,483 --> 00:05:59,819
and I cut there. Better. But the problem is that retention spikes

90
00:05:59,879 --> 00:06:04,699
are lagging. By the time the graph moves, the moment that caused

91
00:06:04,759 --> 00:06:06,658
it was forty seconds ago.

92
00:06:08,086 --> 00:06:12,502
The third attempt actually worked, and it worked because I stopped

93
00:06:12,562 --> 00:06:17,766
trying to find good moments and started trying to find good openings.

94
00:06:18,829 --> 00:06:21,778
Because a clip is not a highlight.

95
00:06:22,210 --> 00:06:26,948
A clip is a cold open. Nobody arrives at your clip having watched

96
00:06:27,008 --> 00:06:28,512
the previous nine minutes.

97
00:06:28,907 --> 00:06:32,612
They arrive mid-scroll with no context and a thumb already moving.

98
00:06:34,048 --> 00:06:39,562
So the only question that matters is: does the first line of this

99
00:06:39,622 --> 00:06:43,072
clip work on somebody who has never heard of you.

100
00:06:44,531 --> 00:06:47,105
And that turns out to be measurable.

101
00:06:47,602 --> 00:06:49,236
Not perfectly. But well enough.

102
00:06:50,392 --> 00:06:57,018
Here is what I look for now, and I built a tool that does this

103
00:06:57,078 --> 00:07:00,562
because I got tired of doing it by hand.

104
00:07:01,513 --> 00:07:05,767
First, does the opening line make a claim or ask a question.

105
00:07:06,242 --> 00:07:08,471
Statements that are neither are dead.

106
00:07:08,820 --> 00:07:11,703
"So anyway, we were talking about" is dead.

107
00:07:12,069 --> 00:07:16,027
"Everybody tells you the algorithm decides who wins" is not dead,

108
00:07:16,087 --> 00:07:21,165
and you will notice I opened this episode with it, because I practice

109
00:07:21,225 --> 00:07:22,951
what I am telling you.

110
00:07:24,008 --> 00:07:25,967
Second, is there an open loop.

111
00:07:26,196 --> 00:07:29,873
Something the sentence sets up and does not immediately close.

112
00:07:30,348 --> 00:07:35,296
Your brain does not let you scroll away from an unclosed loop.

113
00:07:35,597 --> 00:07:39,567
This is not a trick, it is just how attention works.

114
00:07:40,845 --> 00:07:45,202
Third, is there something concrete in the first ten seconds.

115
00:07:45,475 --> 00:07:47,761
A number, a name, a specific thing.

116
00:07:48,031 --> 00:07:51,153
"I lost a lot of money" is nothing.

117
00:07:51,632 --> 00:07:54,299
"It cost me eleven thousand hours" is something.

118
00:07:55,334 --> 00:07:59,384
Fourth, and this is the one people miss, does the clip end.

119
00:07:59,699 --> 00:08:04,564
Not stop. End. There is a difference and the difference is worth

120
00:08:04,624 --> 00:08:07,092
about fifteen percent of your completion rate.

121
00:08:08,539 --> 00:08:12,293
Fifth, can it be understood without the rest of the episode.

122
00:08:12,749 --> 00:08:17,319
If the clip opens with the word "it" and the viewer does not know

123
00:08:17,379 --> 00:08:20,451
what "it" is, you have already lost.

124
00:08:20,835 --> 00:08:24,998
Pronouns without antecedents kill more clips than bad hooks do.

125
00:08:25,747 --> 00:08:30,165
I want to be careful here, because I am describing a system and

126
00:08:30,225 --> 00:08:32,370
systems get treated as laws.

127
00:08:33,653 --> 00:08:36,925
None of this makes a bad recording good.

128
00:08:37,394 --> 00:08:39,748
I want to say that plainly.

129
00:08:40,195 --> 00:08:45,241
If you spent an hour saying nothing, no amount of clipping finds

130
00:08:45,301 --> 00:08:48,693
the moment, because the moment is not in there.

131
00:08:49,117 --> 00:08:51,085
This is amplification, not creation.

132
00:08:52,261 --> 00:08:56,115
But if you did say something real, the difference between that

133
00:08:56,175 --> 00:08:59,502
landing and that disappearing is almost entirely mechanical.

134
00:08:59,995 --> 00:09:02,437
And mechanical problems have mechanical solutions.

135
00:09:03,505 --> 00:09:07,218
The last thing, and then I will let you go.

136
00:09:08,365 --> 00:09:13,008
I get asked constantly what the posting schedule should be.

137
00:09:13,523 --> 00:09:16,641
Three a week, five a week, daily.

138
00:09:16,926 --> 00:09:21,374
And the honest answer is that the schedule matters far less than

139
00:09:21,434 --> 00:09:24,248
whether you can sustain it in month nine.

140
00:09:25,726 --> 00:09:32,088
I burned out twice. Both times were in the fourth month of a five-a-day

141
00:09:32,148 --> 00:09:36,496
sprint. Both times it took about six weeks to want to open the

142
00:09:36,556 --> 00:09:40,807
editor again. If you take the full cost of that, including the

143
00:09:40,867 --> 00:09:45,250
six weeks, five a day is slower than three a week.

144
00:09:46,283 --> 00:09:50,590
So my actual advice, and it is unsatisfying: pick the number you

145
00:09:50,650 --> 00:09:52,506
could do while mildly sick.

146
00:09:52,863 --> 00:09:57,949
That is your number. Not the number you can do when you are inspired.

147
00:09:59,272 --> 00:10:01,195
That is the whole episode.

148
00:10:01,542 --> 00:10:06,059
I will put the clip scoring stuff in the description because a

149
00:10:06,119 --> 00:10:08,128
few people asked. Thanks for listening.
